---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc976.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Historical Retail Land Sales Method — Largely Superseded" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Current Treatment Under ASC 606" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Rescission Periods and Refund Obligations" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — Retail Land Sale Revenue Recognition" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s9", label: "ASC 976 vs IFRS — Comparison" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">High cancellation risk historically drove specialised, now-superseded rules</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">RETAIL LAND SALES — selling individual lots (often via INSTALLMENT contracts) to retail/recreational buyers — historically experienced notably HIGH cancellation and default rates, prompting SPECIALISED, industry-specific revenue recognition criteria distinct from ordinary real estate sales. Under CURRENT guidance, this specialised legacy model has been LARGELY SUPERSEDED by ASC 606's general framework, with COLLECTIBILITY risk now addressed through ASC 606's own general contract-existence and variable consideration mechanisms.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Sellers of individual retail land lots</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 976 applies to entities selling individual parcels of land to retail buyers, typically for recreational or residential use, often financed through seller-provided installment contracts.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Historical Retail Land Sales Method — Largely Superseded</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Bright-line down payment and collectibility thresholds, no longer the operative model</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">PRIOR to ASC 606, retail land sales were governed by a specific recognition method REQUIRING minimum DOWN PAYMENT thresholds (a specific percentage of the sales price), an assessment of the SELLER's remaining performance obligations, and specific COLLECTIBILITY criteria BEFORE full revenue recognition was permitted — reflecting genuine historical concern about high buyer default/cancellation rates in this specific market segment. This SPECIALISED, bright-line method has been LARGELY SUPERSEDED by ASC 606's general model (Section 4).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Current Treatment Under ASC 606</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Control transfer and general collectibility assessment</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Retail land sale revenue is now recognised under ASC 606's GENERAL model — evaluating WHEN control of the lot transfers to the buyer, and assessing COLLECTIBILITY as part of the general CONTRACT EXISTENCE criteria (a contract must be probable of COLLECTION for revenue recognition to begin) — WITHOUT the old bright-line down payment percentage thresholds. Given the historically HIGH default rates in this market, careful COLLECTIBILITY and VARIABLE CONSIDERATION analysis remains especially IMPORTANT in practice, even though the FORMAL bright-line test itself no longer applies.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Rescission Periods and Refund Obligations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A refund liability during any statutory or contractual rescission window</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Many retail land sales are subject to a statutory or contractual RESCISSION period (a window during which the buyer may CANCEL the purchase and receive a refund) — during this period, the seller generally recognises a REFUND LIABILITY for amounts collected, consistent with ASC 606's general treatment of a right of return/rescission, rather than recognising full revenue immediately.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Retail Land Sale Revenue Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Applying the general ASC 606 collectibility assessment</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An installment sale of a recreational lot</div><div class="wk-body">A developer sells a $60,000 recreational land lot via an installment contract, with a $6,000 down payment and the remainder financed over 10 years. Based on the buyer's credit profile and the developer's historical collection experience for similar buyers, collection of substantially all the consideration is deemed PROBABLE.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since collection is deemed PROBABLE (satisfying ASC 606's contract existence criteria) and CONTROL of the lot transfers to the buyer at closing, the developer recognises the FULL $60,000 transaction price as revenue at that point (net of any statutory rescission-period refund liability) — WITHOUT needing to satisfy the OLD bright-line minimum down payment percentage test that would have previously applied.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 976 requires (in addition to general ASC 606 disclosures)</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature of installment sale financing arrangements</span><span>Refund liabilities related to rescission periods</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether collection of consideration is probable, given historically elevated default rates in this market</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Expected rescission/cancellation rates used to estimate refund liabilities</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>ASC 976 vs IFRS — Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 976 vs general IFRS 15 principles</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 976)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 15)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Revenue recognition model</span><span>General ASC 606 control transfer and collectibility assessment</span><span>Same general IFRS 15 model — converged</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Rescission period refund liability</span><span>General right of return treatment</span><span>Same general treatment — converged</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Since the OLD bright-line retail land sales method has been superseded on the US GAAP side, this Topic is now essentially fully CONVERGED with IFRS through the shared ASC 606/IFRS 15 revenue framework.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 976 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Retail land sale revenue</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised upon control transfer, if collection is probable</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Rescission period refund liability</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Recognised during any statutory/contractual cancellation window</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the general control-transfer and collectibility framework now governing retail land sales</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 970</span><span>Real Estate—General — supplies the broader real estate cost capitalisation and allocation framework</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 978</span><span>Real Estate—Time-Sharing Activities — a related, similarly-structured specialised real estate sale Topic</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Rescission period</div><div class="glossary-def">A statutory or contractual window during which a retail land buyer may cancel the purchase and receive a refund.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Retail land sale</div><div class="glossary-def">The sale of an individual land lot to a retail buyer, typically financed through a seller-provided installment contract.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc976 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

